    public event EventHandler<Player>? OnMemberKilled;
    public event EventHandler? OnPartyWiped;

    public Party(IEnumerable<Player> members)
    {   
        Members = new ReadOnlyObservableCollection<Player>(_members);
        foreach(Player player in members)
        {
            AddMember(player);
        }
    }
    public void AddMember(Player player)
    {   
        if(_members.Contains(player)){
            return;
        }
        player.OnKilled += OnPlayerKilled;
        _members.Add(player);

    }
    public void RemoveMember(Player player)
    {   
        if(_members.Remove(player)){
            player.OnKilled -= OnPlayerKilled;
        }
    }
    public void CompleteHealAll()
    {
        foreach(Player character in _members)
        {
            character.CompleteHeal();
        }
    }

    private void OnPlayerKilled(object? sender, System.EventArgs eventArgs)
    {
        if(sender is not Player player)
        {
            return;
        }
        OnMemberKilled?.Invoke(this, player);
        if (isWiped)
        {
            OnPartyWiped?.Invoke(this, System.EventArgs.Empty);
        }
    }
}

content.text:
There are three problems. The first two are in the lines you selected, the third is in the constructor.

**1. `OnPlayerKilled` has the wrong signature (compile error).**
`Party.OnMemberKilled` is an `EventHandler<Player>`, so its handler has to look like `(object? sender, Player player)`. [GameSession.cs:284](Engine.ViewModels/GameSession.cs#L284) still takes `System.EventArgs`, which doesn't match.

There's a second bug in the same method: `sender` is now the **Party**, not the player. So `sender is Player player` would never be true, and the message would never show. The dead player now comes in as the second argument:

```csharp
private void OnPlayerKilled(object? sender, Player player)
{
    _messageBroker.RaiseMessage("");
    _messageBroker.RaiseMessage($"{player.Name} has been killed");
}
```

**2. The property is named `Members` but holds a `Party`.**
That leads to `gameSession.Members.Members[0]`, which is confusing. Name it `Party`. Also name the field `_party`, like the other fields here (`_currentPlayer`, `_currentMonster`):

```csharp
private Party _party = null!;

public Party Party
{
    get { return _party; }
    set
    {
        if (_party != null)
        {
            _party.OnMemberKilled -= OnPlayerKilled;
            _party.OnPartyWiped -= OnGroupKilled;
        }
        _party = value;
        if (_party != null)
        {
            _party.OnMemberKilled += OnPlayerKilled;
            _party.OnPartyWiped += OnGroupKilled;
        }
    }
}
```

**3. The constructor never sets the party, so the game crashes on start.**
[GameSession.cs:113-118](Engine.ViewModels/GameSession.cs#L113-L118) still takes a `Player` and ignores it. `party` is still `null`, so `party.Members[0]` throws a `NullReferenceException`. Take a `Party` and assign it through the **property**, not the field. Only the property's setter subscribes the events:

```csharp
public GameSession(Party party, int xCoordinate, int yCoordinate)
{
    PopulateGameDetails();

    CurrentWorld = WorldFactory.CreateWorld();
    Party = party;
    CurrentPlayer = party.Members[0];
    CurrentLocation = CurrentWorld.LocationAt(xCoordinate, yCoordinate);
}
```

After that change, the callers will no longer compile until you update them to pass `new Party(new[] { player })`:
- [MainWindow.xaml.cs:25](WPFUI/Windows/MainWindow.xaml.cs#L25)
- [TestGameSession.cs:12](Test/ViewModels/TestGameSession.cs#L12)
- [TestGameSession.cs:45](Test/ViewModels/TestGameSession.cs#L45)

